10. RESPONSIVE DESIGN & iPHONE REQUIREMENTS
========================================================

Develop desktop and mobile as first-class experiences.

Test at representative viewport sizes:
- Desktop: 1920x1080 and 1440x900
- Laptop: 1366x768
- iPhone portrait: 390x844 and 393x852
- iPhone landscape: approximately 844x390
- Small mobile: 360x740

Account for device pixel ratio, orientation, safe areas, dynamic browser bars, and touch controls.

Require:
- No horizontal overflow
- No clipped primary buttons or critical elements
- No unintentionally hidden game objects
- Touch targets approximately 44x44 CSS pixels or larger
- Readable overlays
- Responsive camera framing
- Clear gesture affordances
- Appropriate pause/resume behavior
- Recovered rendering after browser tab suspension
- Correct handling of WebGL context loss where feasible

Consider landscape mode where gameplay benefits from it, but ensure portrait users receive a useful and attractive experience.

Mobile must not look like a broken, reduced-size desktop application.

========================================================
11. PERFORMANCE ENGINEERING
========================================================

Aim for these measurable performance objectives on defined, representative hardware:

DESKTOP:
- Target: 60 FPS at 1920x1080.
- Frame budget: approximately 16.7 ms.
- Target smooth camera motion and interactions.
- Higher refresh rates are optional enhancements.

MOBILE:
- Target: 60 FPS on capable modern phones using adaptive quality.
- Minimum practical target: sustained 30 FPS on representative supported phones.
- 30 FPS frame budget: approximately 33.3 ms.
- Avoid pronounced input latency, thermal instability, or repeated frame stalls.

These are engineering targets, not guarantees independent of hardware.

IMPLEMENT:
- Adaptive device pixel ratio
- Low, medium, and high quality profiles
- Automatic performance regression when frame times worsen
- Selective shadow-quality reduction
- Reduced postprocessing on lower tiers
- Instanced meshes for repeated objects
- Geometry and material reuse
- Texture compression where supported
- Mesh compression where appropriate
- Object culling and level of detail
- Lazy loading of noncritical assets
- Resource disposal and cleanup
- Efficient animation loops
- GPU-efficient particle effects

Avoid per-frame React state updates for high-frequency 3D transformations.

Use references, useFrame, direct Three.js mutation, shader uniforms, or appropriate simulation state systems.

Avoid unnecessary allocations in frame loops.

Instrument performance rather than assuming an optimization is effective.

Record:
- Median FPS
- 1% low FPS or equivalent frame-time tail metric
- Approximate draw calls
- Triangle counts where useful
- Texture and asset transfer sizes
- JavaScript heap trends where measurable
- Startup and scene-ready times
- Browser console errors

Measure on actual devices where accessible.

Do not claim physical iPhone performance has been verified if testing only occurred through desktop mobile emulation.

Prioritize consistent frame delivery and responsive interaction over maximal graphical complexity.

