16. IMPLEMENTATION STRATEGY — AVOID TIMEOUTS
========================================================

Work methodically in bounded phases.

PHASE 1: REPOSITORY AUDIT
Inspect the codebase, identify constraints, preserve existing features, and document a concise technical plan.

PHASE 2: ART DIRECTION
Define the visual direction, scene composition, asset plan, animation language, and measurable acceptance criteria.

PHASE 3: GRAPHICS FOUNDATION
Implement camera, lighting, renderer configuration, materials, environment, and rendering architecture.

PHASE 4: SCENE & ASSET PRODUCTION
Create or integrate polished scene geometry, environment details, and visual composition.

PHASE 5: ANIMATION & INTERACTION
Implement meaningful motion, responsive controls, physical behavior, and feedback.

PHASE 6: POSTPROCESSING & CINEMATIC POLISH
Add and tune shadows, atmosphere, color, effects, and final visual details.

PHASE 7: MOBILE OPTIMIZATION
Implement mobile-specific layout, camera framing, adaptive quality, and touch controls.

PHASE 8: TESTING & VISUAL IMPROVEMENT
Run tests, inspect screenshots, evaluate performance, fix defects, and refine.

PHASE 9: PRODUCTION READINESS
Verify production builds, document findings and limitations, and prepare deployment.

Maintain:
- docs/3D_VISUAL_STANDARD.md
- docs/3D_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md
- docs/3D_QUALITY_REPORT.md

Update the implementation plan as work progresses.

If Git is configured, create logical commits after completed, validated phases. Do not commit secrets, generated caches, or unnecessary binaries.

Do not attempt a massive one-shot rewrite when smaller validated steps are more reliable.

Do not abandon visual quality simply because the scene is technically functional.

